          On thinking about thinking, I would suggest looking at some
composition theory.  Like any other field, it is dominated by
blowhards who publish phatically, but the premise of most of these
theories is that if we can understand how the mind works naturally,
then we can understand what it is exactly that people do when they
write. As it is, much composition theory is based in Aristolelian
logic and rhetorical theory.  In fact, if you look at your old
composition handbooks, you will find that they are categorized
(inadvertently) to correspond with the master tropes: metaphor,
metonymy, synecdoche and irony; many argue that we think naturally in
these terms.  These four tropes correspond with many other series of
4, such as Piaget's stages of cognitive developmental; political
positions (anarchistic, radical, liberal, conservative); approaches to
life in general (formist, mechanist, organicist, contextual); I've
found them in Freud, Jung and even Crowley.  They're everywhere, in
one form or another.  That is just one example of what you can find in
comp. theory.  What interests me more are the heuristics, which are
methods of invention or discovery (ways of learning what you think).
For instance, Young, Becker and Pike devised the Tagmemic Heuristic,
which is based on field theory in physics.  On the whole, I think that
these theories are useless in the sense that they are intended
(teaching writing).  They do, however, offer some interesting insights
into our thought processes. If you have access to the curent MLA
bibliography at your university library, you might look under
composition theory and then add to it whatever you are interested in:
cognitive process, heuristics, whatever.  If you get into this, it
might provide you with some leads on more detailed information in
psychology, philosophy, ect.
